<article class="env-corollary" id="02BW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02BW">Corollary <span data-tag="02BW">4.4.2.12</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a conservative functor of $\infty $-categories and let $q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then the induced functors </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  F_{q/}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{q/} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{(F \circ q)/} \quad \quad F_{/q}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/q} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{/(F \circ q)} \]
</div>
<p> are also conservative. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will show that the functor $F_{/q}$ is conservative; the conservativity of $F_{q/}$ follows by a similar argument. Let $\pi : \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/q} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\pi ': \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{/(F \circ q)} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ denote the projection maps. Then $\pi $ and $\pi '$ are right fibrations of $\infty $-categories (Proposition <a data-tag="018F" href="/tag/018F">4.3.6.1</a>), and therefore conservative (Proposition <a data-tag="019K" href="/tag/019K">4.4.2.11</a>). Since $F$ is conservative, from Remark <a data-tag="019J" href="/tag/019J">4.4.2.10</a> that the functor $F \circ \pi = \pi ' \circ F_{/q}$ is also conservative. Applying Remark <a data-tag="019J" href="/tag/019J">4.4.2.10</a> again, we conclude that $F_{/q}$ is conservative. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
